2^31-1 is the maximum positive value for a 32-bit signed integer.

> :D That score isn't going to be beat, ever.

I'm sure there are a lot of C programmers out there that really,
*really* wish that were universally true :P

The maximum value of a signed long int is *at least* 2,147,483,647 for
a C99-compliant compiler. It can be larger without breaking the
standard, and all bets are off for non-standard compilers. As far as I
know, all the major C compilers for the x64 platform use the "LP64"
model, meaning that longs and pointers are 64 bits wide. This is
compliant with, but not required by the C99 standard.

I've heard that there are some x64 nethack linux binaries floating
around somewhere, but I haven't seen what was done to the source to
make that happen. If the score is still declared as a long int in the
64-bit binaries' source, the maximum score is likely 2^63-1 (roughly
9.2 quintillion).

Incidentally, why is the score a signed value in the first place? That
seems...odd. Short of wrapping the counter, I'm not aware of any way to
lose points in nethack, but maybe I just haven't tried hard enough :P



